11:28:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: This comes down to the different physical processes at work—heat alone isn't what causes shrinkage. **Why hot washing shrinks fabric:** Shrinkage in fibers like cotton and wool happens due to a combination of: - **Heat** - **Moisture** (usually a lot of it, sustained over time) - **Agitation** (the mechanical action of the washing machine) When fibers get wet, they swell and the individual strands can relax out of the tension they were held in during manufacturing (weaving/knitting fabric stretches fibers slightly). Hot water accelerates this relaxation process. Then the agitation from washing causes the fibers to tangle and mat together more tightly as they dry, locking in a more compact, shrunken structure. Wool is especially prone to this because its scaled fiber structure causes felting when agitated while wet and warm. **Why ironing at high heat doesn't cause the same shrinkage:** Ironing involves: - **High heat**, but applied briefly and directly - **Minimal moisture** (just enough to help release wrinkles, if using steam) - **No agitation**—you're pressing, not tumbling or scrubbing Without the agitation and without prolonged saturation, the fibers don't get the same opportunity to felt, tangle, or relax into a new tighter configuration. The heat from an iron mainly works on relaxing the fibers just enough to smooth out wrinkles (breaking and reforming some of the hydrogen bonds in the fibers), but it does so with the fabric under tension (from your pulling and the ironing board) rather than left loose and free to contract. **The key insight:** It's not heat by itself that shrinks clothes—it's the *combination* of heat + moisture + mechanical action (or sometimes heat + moisture + lack of tension) that causes fibers to reorganize into a shrunken state. Ironing skips the moisture-saturation and agitation part, and also applies tension, so the same "high heat" doesn't have the same effect. That said, this is why ironing settings for delicate items still often warn to use steam cautiously—if you introduce a lot of moisture *and* let fabric shrink back without pulling it taut, you can cause some shrinkage even during ironing.
